course: dictionary
lesson: 11753
title: Banishment
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Banishment is a noun. It means being sent away from your country or home as a punishment, and not being allowed to come back. Its verb is banish, which means to send someone away.
explain: «The prince was given banishment and had to leave the kingdom forever.» The clues are leave, forever and kingdom. Someone who must leave a kingdom forever is being sent away, so banishment must mean that.
explain: The word can also mean getting rid of something completely. «The banishment of the cobwebs took all morning.» Swap in «getting rid of» and the sentence still makes sense, so that is the second meaning.
fact: Banishment means being sent away from your country or home as a punishment.
fact: Banishment is a noun. Its verb is banish.
fact: Banishment can also mean getting rid of something completely.
fact: In history, kings punished their enemies with banishment.
fact: In stories, a prince is banished from the kingdom, and in the Bible, Adam and Eve were sent out of the Garden of Eden.
exercise: «The prince was given banishment and must never return to the kingdom.» What does banishment mean here? | A big party to welcome him home | *Being sent away as a punishment and not allowed back | A short holiday by the sea | A gift from the king
exercise: «After the storm, the banishment of the grey clouds made the sky bright.» What does banishment mean here? | Carrying the clouds home | Making the clouds bigger | Watching the clouds slowly grow | *Getting rid of the clouds completely
exercise: Which word means being sent away from home as a punishment? | garden | *banishment | feast | kingdom
test: «Ali was given banishment to his room for an hour, which made his family laugh.» What does this show? | *A funny, pretend punishment, not a real one | A birthday present | Being sent away from the country forever | A very long holiday
test: What kind of word is banishment? | A question word | A verb | *A noun | An adjective
exam: Which sentence uses banishment to mean getting rid of something completely? | *«The banishment of the dust took all afternoon.» | «She took banishment on her holiday in Spain.» | «The banishment of the king was announced at breakfast.» | «The banishment of the prince made the people sad.»
